  <div class="section-label">Noise Abatement Policy</div>
  <h2>Federal Highway Administration Noise Criteria and Sound Wall Eligibility</h2>

  <p>UDOT's noise policy is governed by the Federal Highway Administration Noise Abatement Criteria and Utah Administrative Code R930-3.{% cite "utah-r930-3-noise" %} A traffic noise impact is officially identified when predicted noise levels equal or exceed <strong>66 decibels (dBA)</strong> for residential areas (Activity Category B).</p>

  <p>When an impact is identified, UDOT evaluates mitigation measures — primarily sound walls — against two criteria:</p>

  <ul style="margin: 16px 0 20px 24px; line-height: 2.2;">
    <li><strong>Feasibility:</strong> The wall must achieve a minimum 5 dBA noise reduction for impacted receivers.{% cite "utah-r930-3-noise" %}</li>
    <li><strong>Reasonableness:</strong> The wall must be cost-effective (typically evaluated per-benefited-residence) and must receive majority approval through a <strong>community balloting process</strong>.{% cite "udot-wdc-phase2-noise" %}</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Residents within approximately <strong>500 feet of the highway centerline</strong> are typically evaluated for noise impacts under current UDOT practice. UDOT's policy also counts an impact when future noise is at least 10 dBA above existing levels, and treats a wall as feasible only if it cuts noise by at least 5 dBA for at least half of the front-row receivers, with a design goal of 7 dBA for at least 35% of them.{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol1-nov2026" %}</p>

  <h3>Weber County Segment: Draft State Environmental Study Noise Results</h3>

  <p>The Draft State Environmental Study (November 6, 2026) modeled 620 noise-sensitive locations between 1800 North and 4000 South. With the Preferred Alternative, <strong>324 would be impacted</strong> by traffic noise, and <strong>234 of those would see a substantial increase</strong> of 10 dBA or more over today's levels. Modeled levels range from 30–71 dBA today to 40–75 dBA with the freeway.{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol1-nov2026" %}</p>

  <p>UDOT evaluated 15 noise walls and recommends three for final design and balloting:{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol1-nov2026" %}{% cite "udot-draft-ses-appendix-nov2026" %}</p>

